WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.903
<v Examiner>The three layers of the eye.

2
00:00:02.903 --> 00:00:04.077
<v Examiner>Four and a half marks.

3
00:00:04.527 --> 00:00:07.180
<v Examiner>What are the three layers, or tunics, of the eye?

4
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.455
<v Examiner>Give their structures and their functions.

5
00:00:09.905 --> 00:00:14.235
The hook, from the outside in: the tough coat, the blood coat, and the seeing coat.

6
00:00:14.685 --> 00:00:17.766
The tough coat is the fibrous tunic, on the outside.

7
00:00:17.766 --> 00:00:18.773
It has two parts.

8
00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:23.394
The white sclera, which protects the eye and anchors the muscles that move it.

9
00:00:23.394 --> 00:00:26.652
And the clear cornea at the front, which lets light in,

10
00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:29.555
and does about eighty per cent of the refraction.

11
00:00:30.005 --> 00:00:34.394
The blood coat is the vascular tunic, also called the uvea, in the middle.

12
00:00:34.394 --> 00:00:35.521
It has three parts.

13
00:00:35.521 --> 00:00:38.427
The choroid: pigmented and rich in blood vessels.

14
00:00:38.427 --> 00:00:41.867
It nourishes the other layers, and stops light scattering.

15
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:47.027
The ciliary body: its ciliary muscles and suspensory ligaments hold the lens and change

16
00:00:47.027 --> 00:00:50.171
its shape, and its processes make the aqueous humour.

17
00:00:50.171 --> 00:00:54.679
And the iris: its circular and radial muscles control the size of the pupil,

18
00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:56.458
and so how much light gets in.

19
00:00:57.908 --> 00:01:02.017
The seeing coat is the neural tunic, the retina, on the inside.

20
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:06.191
Its outer pigmented layer absorbs light and stops it scattering.

21
00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:11.865
Its inner neural layer has three layers of neurons: the photoreceptors, rods and cones,

22
00:01:11.865 --> 00:01:17.473
then bipolar cells, then ganglion cells, whose axons leave the eye as the optic nerve,

23
00:01:17.473 --> 00:01:18.647
cranial nerve two.

24
00:01:18.647 --> 00:01:21.908
The photoreceptors turn light into nerve impulses.

25
00:01:23.358 --> 00:01:24.030
The traps.

26
00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:29.208
The cornea, not the lens, does most of the refraction: about eighty per cent.

27
00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:34.184
Second: the choroid belongs to the middle, vascular tunic, not the retina.

28
00:01:34.184 --> 00:01:37.546
Third: the rods and cones are in the neural layer.

29
00:01:37.546 --> 00:01:40.169
The pigmented layer only absorbs light.

30
00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:41.158
<v Examiner>Once more.

31
00:01:41.158 --> 00:01:42.667
<v Examiner>The three layers of the eye.

32
00:01:45.117 --> 00:01:49.566
The fibrous tunic: the sclera, which protects the eye and anchors its muscles,

33
00:01:49.566 --> 00:01:53.387
and the cornea, which does about eighty per cent of the refraction.

34
00:01:53.387 --> 00:01:57.950
The vascular tunic: the choroid, rich in blood vessels, which nourishes the eye;

35
00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:02.741
the ciliary body, which holds the lens, changes its shape, and makes aqueous humour;

36
00:02:02.741 --> 00:02:05.023
and the iris, which controls pupil size.

37
00:02:05.023 --> 00:02:10.042
The neural tunic, the retina: a pigmented layer that absorbs light, then photoreceptors,

38
00:02:10.042 --> 00:02:15.175
rods and cones, then bipolar cells, then ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve.
